---
title: Paris, Texas
subject: dir. Wim Wenders
genre: Film
rating: 5
date: 2026-08-27
excerpt: >-
  A red baseball cap, a wall of glass, and the longest silence in American
  cinema. Devastating and unhurried.
---

A man walks out of the desert with no memory he is willing to share, and for the first twenty minutes he does not speak at all. Wenders lets that silence sit. Travis is not mysterious in the way films usually mean it — he is simply a person who has decided that speaking would cost more than he can pay, and the film is patient enough to wait until he changes his mind.

Robby Müller shoots Texas as a series of saturated, empty rectangles: motel signs, highway overpasses, a used-car lot at dusk. Nothing is beautiful by accident here, and nothing is beautiful in a way that lets you off the hook. The landscape keeps insisting there is room to disappear, which is exactly what Travis did.

> I knew these people. These two people. They were in love with each other.

Then the peep-show scene, which is one of the few sequences in film I would call structurally perfect. Two people separated by one-way glass, one of them narrating their marriage in the third person because the first person is unbearable. Nastassja Kinski watches a reflection that is really her own face; Harry Dean Stanton talks to a wall. It is the whole film compressed into a single piece of furniture.

The ending refuses the reunion you have spent two hours earning. That refusal is the point. Wenders is not interested in whether love survives — he is interested in what a person owes the people they damaged, and whether showing up is enough. It isn't. He shows up anyway.
